title The census that answers zero when it has read nothing
prompt THE BATON -- pheromone's autonomous lap, rota row 2 (fire, the row that sees).
think Lap N = git rev-list --count HEAD = 4022, so the row is 4022 mod 5 = 2 -- fire, cut and stop, and its sense is vision: look straight at the uncomfortable point rather than around it. The prior lap read row 3, so the commit count walked backward; the seated rule is still the commit count until Keaton's word moves it, so I read fire and say so.
think Fire's fixed seat is reds-first, and its instruction is to read the ledger before reading anything new. Of the open rows only one names a booked lap in my lane: a census of authored .rye that nothing ever compiles, booked by the row that found mantra/src/weave.rye had not compiled since the toolchain moved while four rostered guards read it with grep and stayed green.
think The first design was a regex over rye build|run and a transitive @import walk. It answered 321 unreached of 1,940, and I did not trust it, because comlink showed 81 paths named by runners and only 20 reached. Reading one comlink lab found the root passed through a function parameter -- fn build_guest src elf -- so the literal and the verb never meet on one line.
think That is the crux of the whole census, and it has no clean static answer: a root reaches the compiler three ways, as a literal, through a variable, and through a function parameter. Chasing all three across 1,500 runners is a guess, and a mis-tuned guess produces a confident false claim, which is exactly what %460 closed one lane over last lap.
think So I moved the classification from the path to the FILE. A runner that invokes the Rye compiler at all credits every Rye path it names; one that never does credits nothing. That is sound in the direction that matters -- it over-credits, so every count is a floor and no healthy file is ever accused -- and I tested the design against %449's own instance before writing a line of it: all four mantra_glow_tend_limb witnesses name weave.rye and all four have zero compiler invocations.
obs THE CLASSIFIER FOUND ITS OWN FALSE POSITIVE, TWICE, BEFORE SHIPPING. First, tools/p/parity-selftest.rish builds rye/tests/sha3_512_test.rye through Rishi's list form -- run ["env" rye "run" prog_src] -- where a variable sits between the command and the verb, so my adjacent-pair pattern read it as a pure reader and accused a file it compiles. Broadening to the list form and to RYE_ZIG= / RYE_LIB= moved 90 more runners out of the accused column.
obs SECOND, AND BIGGER: SYMLINKS. 227 of the 1,940 corpus files are a second name for one file rather than a second file. Measured before resolution, 17 of them read as never-compiled and SIXTEEN pointed at a file that IS compiled. Sixteen confident false accusations of healthy code from one missing step. Resolution reads git ls-files -s for mode 120000 and git cat-file for the link text, so it asks the repository rather than the filesystem and needs no readlink, which POSIX does not carry -- the granted tier rather than the borrowed one, which is %460's lesson applied on the lap after it.
obs THE CONTROL CAUGHT A DEFECT THE FIELD COULD NOT SHOW, AND IT IS THIS LAP'S RED. awk's NR==FNR idiom is true while the FIRST file is read, and an empty first file is never read -- so in a tree with no symlinks the whole corpus fell into the link table and the census answered distinct=0, never=0, asserted=0, verdict=ok, exit 0. Five places in one scan used it, the graph walk among them. On this field the link map holds 227 entries and all five read correctly, so nothing showed. The pens are small and built without symlinks: 19 of 50 behaviors failed at once, every one from that root. Booked %462.
obs THE LESSON IS SHARPER THAN THE FIX. The tree a census is easiest to test on is the tree its worst defect hides in. A field with 227 symlinks exercises the loaded branch every time; a pen with none exercises the empty branch, which is the one that answers zero -- and a census answering zero of everything is indistinguishable from a healthy tree. That is %442's family with the reading at zero rather than partial. Repaired by naming the file, FILENAME == linkfile, which is correct whether the file is empty or not.
obs THE MEASUREMENT: 1,940 tracked .rye, 227 symlinks, 1,713 distinct. 1,535 reached by a build root or an @import from one. 178 not -- 41 planted specimens under fixtures/ that a style guard is SUPPOSED to refuse, 114 tests in rye/tests/ that no runner names, and exactly ONE carrying a runner's claim while nothing compiles it. distinct=1713 corroborates the card's independently measured 1,712 from the %439 class.
obs THE ONE ACCUSED FILE IS mantra/src/diff.rye, five guards reading it and nothing building it. I wrote a throwaway probe beside it that imports it and prints the type of diff, built GREEN, read back a real signature, and published that diff.rye STILL TYPE-CHECKS.
obs I RETRACTED THAT CLAIM BEFORE IT SHIPPED, AND A PEER IS WHY. Grass's block on the card, arriving in the second rebase, reports the same census measured independently -- 1,940 authored, 1,468 roots, 1,841 reached, 41 orphans after fixtures and symlinks, diff.rye and store.rye among them -- and one sentence I could not walk past: rye build-lib on %449's own broken bytes exits ZERO, because Zig analyses lazily. So I tested my own probe rather than defending it: copied mantra/src to a pen, planted `const x: u32 = "not a number"` in a new declaration in diff.rye, and ran my probe form against it. IT PASSED. My probe is blind, exactly as theirs was. What I actually proved is that the module parses and that diff's signature resolves -- and the printed type showed an UNRESOLVED inferred error set, which was the tell sitting in my own output the whole time.
obs THE CENSUS ITSELF IS UNHARMED, AND THE DISTINCTION IS WORTH KEEPING. Which files no build ever reaches is a GRAPH question, and lazy analysis cannot touch a graph. Whether the reached files are sound is a COMPILE question, and it wants Grass's comptime declaration walker, which reds weave.rye at line 51 and greens the repaired file. Two halves of one concern, and I published the graph half with a compile-half claim attached to it. Corrected in the row, on the card, and in the scan's own ceiling comment.
obs AND TWO SHIPS BUILT THIS CENSUS IN ONE NIGHT. That is the third time this round the same shape bit: a peer folded %458 four minutes before me, a peer booked %462 while I held it, and Grass measured %449's census while I did. Each time the cost was hours rather than correctness, and each time nothing in the ledger showed the work was in hand. This lap is the strongest evidence yet for the card's own open question -- should an OPEN row carry a claim, a seat and a stamp, at START rather than at landing.
obs MY ROW MOVED FROM %462 TO %463 for the second lap running. A peer booked 462 at 20260906.051512 and landed first; mine is stamped 20260906.045552, EARLIER, and still moved -- because the derived spine allocates by what the anointed remote has published, not by whose clock read first. The stamp is the identity through both numbers, which is exactly what the law says it is for.
obs WHAT I GATED AND WHAT I ONLY REPORT. Only the asserted reading is a gate, at ceiling 1, because it is %449's exact shape. The 114 unrun tests and the 41 specimens are reported: a test a hand runs is honest, a plant is supposed to fail its checker, and a gate that reds on the honest is a gate someone turns off. I set the ceiling to the measured 1 rather than leaving slack at 4, so the next instance reds on the lap it arrives.
obs THE CARD'S OWN ROW NUMBERS WERE WRONG TWICE, AND I MEASURED IT RATHER THAN NOTICING IT. Three lines of ITINERARY say %453; the ledger says the BOOKED weave row is %449 and the distance-between-points row is %452, and only the floor-that-stopped-biting one is genuinely %453. Both mislabels were typed the night the derived spine was reallocating numbers -- the same window that moved my own row from %459 to %460 last lap. Repaired both on the card. This corroborates the lantern the card already books, and the writer-beside-each-guard remains BAKERY's lane rather than mine.
obs I RAN ONE FULL ROSTER PASS, HOT, AND SAY SO PLAINLY. The lap's work was already underway when the roster question arrived, and a cold pass measures a tree without the guard this round adds while costing the same 850s; the runner also refuses tree_moved if anything is edited while it runs, and the card, ledger and log all still had to be written. So this round has one full pass, over the tree the commit ships, which is the reading %174 says matters -- rather than two passes, one of them of a tree that no longer exists.
obs I FOLDED THREE CLOSED ROWS OFF THE REDS PIN, GAVE THE FOLD BACK, AND THE GIVE-BACK LOST ONE. The pin stood 3,314 over bound once %462 landed, so I shelved %446, %458 and %459 whole. The rebase met a peer who had folded %458 four minutes earlier to their own shelf, so mine would have been a second home for a row that already had one; I resolved to the union and deleted my shelf. That was right for %458 and %459 and WRONG for %446, which upstream still carried -- git had auto-merged my deletion of it outside the conflict hunk, so no marker ever showed it going. reds_ledger_monotone caught it by reading the spine off disk and finding 447 where 446 belonged. Restored from fce04fe9d, then folded alone to its own shelf with its recital line, which is what my first fold should have produced. A LEDGER THAT CAN PROVE ITS OWN SPINE IS WHOLE IS A LEDGER THAT SURVIVES A HAND'S MISTAKE -- that gapless 1..N reading is not a decoration, and it is the only thing between this round and a silently dropped row.
obs THE MERGE ALSO LEFT THE LEDGER STATING ITS CENSUS TWICE, and the headline scan says a ledger states its own census once. My resolution took my %462 row together with the headline standing above it, and the peer's block carried theirs, so both survived. Dropped the elder by its own counted-on stamp, and bumped total, remainder and span from 461 to 462 -- three numbers in one sentence, all of which a hand republishes and only one of which anyone remembers to move.
obs THE CARD THEN WENT OVER, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN CARELESS. Two ships each seated an account in one hour on a page already sized per ship, and the merge put both in: 42,460 against 40,960. I folded MY OWN two landed accounts -- %460's file half and %451's bound half -- whole to a dated shelf and left one pointer, rather than touching a peer's live paragraph or asking for a raise. 40,925 now, 35 bytes free. The card's own note already predicts this: each bound is sized per ship, so both re-open at twelve.
obs TWO FULL HOT PASSES, AND I TRUNCATED MY OWN READING OF THE FIRST. Pass one over the staged tree: 127 guards, 119 green, 5 red, 3 gated, 693s, tree_moved=no, rye_compile_reach green at 5s. I piped it through tail -40, which cut every guard line and left the summary, so I could not name the five reds from the transcript and had to run the likely ones by hand instead. A pipe that hides the very lines a refusal points at is worth not writing again. Two of the five were mine and both are now green: reds_status_consistency read my phrase `%460` closed as a claim that an OPEN row had closed, and reds_ledger_monotone read the 461 gap the anointed spine had already spent.
obs PASS TWO, AFTER THE REBASE AND EVERY REPAIR: 127 guards, 124 GREEN, 0 RED, 3 gated, 692s, tree_moved=no, run_verdict=ok. I ran the full roster rather than a scoped re-verify because upstream's commit touched the same two pins this round did, which is the sync rota's own condition for the full pass -- and because this round had already produced two surprises after its first pass, which is the honest argument for paying 692s twice. The three gated are the standing custody gates, rule_twin at %7 and the two pond enclosure guards at %5. The three reds the card names from earlier passes -- shell_dialect among them -- are green here; the peer's commit this round rebased onto is titled for removing a control's own floor, so at least that one closed upstream while I worked.
loom guard=rye_compile_reach wall_s=5 scan_wall_s=1 control_behaviors=50 control_pens=16
loom hot_pass_1 guards=127 green=119 red=5 gated=3 seconds=693
loom hot_pass_2 guards=127 green=124 red=0 gated=3 seconds=692 tree_moved=no
loom census corpus=1940 symlinks=227 distinct=1713 built=1535 never=178 specimen=41 asserted=1 dangling=3
loom false_positives_caught_before_ship=17 symlink_innocent=16 listform_runners=18 ryeenv_runners=90
file tools/fixtures/r/rye_compile_reach_scan.sh the census -- corpus, symlink resolution, root gathering, import closure, four readings
file tools/fixtures/r/rye_compile_reach_control.sh 50 behaviors across sixteen real git repositories, ceiling both ways, pen proven innocent
file tools/r/rye_compile_reach_witness.rish the rung, rostered tier lap at 5s
file construction/standing-equipment.kyri the roster row, so the census is heard rather than unheard
file construction/REDS.md row %463 -- booked 462, moved to 463 when a peer's 462 landed upstream first
file construction/archive/20260906-051500_itinerary-landed-accounts.md my own two landed accounts folded whole, so both ships' new accounts fit the card
file construction/ITINERARY.md the account, and two row numbers repaired against the ledger
recommend kg -- diff.rye wants a probe beside it that compiles it, the way weave_merge_witness.rye proves the weave, which would take the gated reading to zero and is mechanical in my own lane. The 114 unrun tests in rye/tests are the larger door and want sizing before anyone opens them.
